# @@NAME@@

@@DESCRIPTION@@

This generated OpenStudio SI plugin includes a starter skill and optional MCP
example. Replace the layout demonstration and cases with reviewed domain work.
Local generation requires no running OpenStudio service or GitHub repository.

## Local development

Install the shared developer tools from the registered `openstudio-si` PyPI package:

```bash
python -m pip install "openstudio-si[plugin-dev]"
openstudio-si plugin check-project .
openstudio-si plugin validate ./plugin
mkdir -p dist
openstudio-si plugin export ./plugin --output ./dist/plugin > dist/export-report.json
openstudio-si plugin check-hosts dist/export-report.json
```

Use a release that includes these commands; older releases do not provide the
extra. The installation includes the OpenStudio SDK and runtime dependencies.
No separate tooling download or lab-managed tooling lock is required. Native
host checks also require Claude Code and Codex. CI maintains their versions
centrally and installs them with npm --ignore-scripts.

@@MCP_SETUP@@

If using the example server, install this project's own Python runtime in your
chosen environment with `python -m pip install -e .`. This local build does not
install the tooling by an unregistered name and is not run in the upload job.

## CI setup and upgrades

The initial workflow files are manual-only configuration stubs. After PNNL
publishes an `openstudio-si` release containing plugin development tools and
reviews the shared workflows, activate them with:

```bash
openstudio-si plugin configure-ci . \
  --workflow-ref <full-PNNL-workflow-commit-SHA> \
  --tooling-version <published-openstudio-si-version>
```

The command installs no packages. It records the exact stable PyPI version and
writes thin calls to the pinned PNNL workflows. The version defaults to the
installed `openstudio-si` version (minimum 0.5.0). CI probes the required
commands before proceeding. Run it again with a newer ref/version to
upgrade without recreating this project. Edited managed workflows require
reconciliation before upgrading. Commit the resulting files.

The read-only build installs `openstudio-si[plugin-dev]==<version>` from PyPI
in a clean environment with binary wheels. Dependencies resolve for the selected
platform/Python; this pins the tool release, not every transitive dependency.
Checkout uses persist-credentials: false. The separate upload-only job has
contents: write, performs no checkout or dependency installation, and uploads
candidate artifacts. No model credentials or LLM calls are used.

## Identity, license and evaluation

Review owner, maintainers and dependencies in plugin/openstudio-si-plugin.yaml.
The license was explicitly selected during generation, and its copyright holder
uses the supplied holder or organization/maintainer. The template does not decide
an approved default for a lab; confirm the choice with its legal/IP office.

The manifest version is also the Python build version. Hatch reads it using its
[regex version source](https://hatch.pypa.io/latest/plugins/version-source/regex/).
Keep schema/API ranges separate from plugin release numbers. Evaluation case and
run configuration are draft 0.1. Use the local runner below after selecting your models and credential environment variables. Judge scores remain advisory.

## Marketplace release

Before tagging, run plugin check-project . --require-tracked locally. CI cannot
recover ignored files that never reached Git. The same check in a fresh CI
checkout is only a consistency check.

After CI setup, publish a source GitHub release with tag v<manifest-version>.
The shared build validates tracked source, checks hosts, verifies the tag, and
creates reproducible archives plus hashes/source provenance. The upload-only job
stages those assets on this repository's release. Re-runs use --clobber so a
partial earlier upload can be completed. The intended destination is a
plugin-scoped PR in pnnl/openstudio-si-plugins, using the central importer.
That automatic submission is step E and is explicitly still pending. There is
no lab PyPI release and no shared-marketplace write token in the build job.

Generation provenance is in .openstudio-si-template.json; CI setup is recorded
in .openstudio-si/ci.json (workflow commit and tool release).

The SDK is installed with `openstudio-si[plugin-dev]`, but generation and
packaging do not import it or require a native OpenStudio CLI. Budget roughly
370 MB for the environment; size varies with platform and dependencies. The
pinned OpenStudio 3.11.0 package currently has no Windows Python 3.14 wheel;
use a supported Python/platform combination (CI uses Linux/Python 3.13).


## Local agent evaluation with DeepEval

Install `python -m pip install "openstudio-si[eval]"`. Edit the generated
`eval-run.example.yaml`, select agent/judge models and the DeepEval judge supplier, set credential
ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES, and save as `eval-run.local.yaml`. Export first, then run:

```bash
openstudio-si eval run --plugin ./plugin --cases ./plugin/evals/cases \
  --config ./eval-run.local.yaml --output ./outputs/evals/run-001
openstudio-si eval report --run ./outputs/evals/run-001
```

Plugin paths are relative to the config file. Cases and run/result contracts are
draft 0.1. The first host drivers use API/gateway credentials in isolated state,
not existing subscription login files. Claude needs explicit allowed_tools for
shell/edit tasks. Codex currently cannot verify skill activation through exec
JSONL; those checks are marked not_observable with notes. Codex defaults to
read-only; opt into agent.sandbox: workspace-write for edits. Host capability
profiles are recorded, and MCP policies remain plugin-defined. Text model checks do not validate
sizing; metrics quoted in prose are unverified notes. SQL/binary content
extraction is not implemented; a metric requiring file contents must declare
required_artifacts and include context. Keep reviewed references out of inputs. Cases configure G-Eval criteria or steps, evidence parameters and optional score bands. Judge scores stay advisory. Keep all actual LLM execution out of CI;
unit-testing the framework with local stubs requires no model credentials.

Use `expected.output` for literal answers instead of treating the answer string as
a private reference document. Single-line tool-result overlap is an advisory
warning next to the score; answer-only matches have lower confidence. A whole
private reference in tool results, or a strict majority of its distinct substantial
private lines (at least two), fails the session and skips judging. Substantial
means at least 32 normalized characters; whole-reference matching uses the same
minimum. Text present in the prompt, inputs or installed plugin files is excluded.
Explicit evaluation-path access also fails the session and skips judging.
`summary.json` includes a total `warning_count` across sessions.

Write references in your own words as independently reviewed expectations; do not
paste plugin tool output into them. A correct call to the same tool can otherwise
match the whole reference and fail the session. Use `expected.output` for literal
answers or `expected.model` checks for supported model properties. Rewording reduces
accidental matches but cannot guarantee that the audit identifies the text's source.
Short table rows such as `Core | 41.2` are below the content threshold: a whole-table
read can be detected, while reads split across tool results may produce no content
finding. The heuristic is not complete answer-key protection.

Hosted judges receive selected evaluation data, potentially including full tool
traces, reference text and OSM/project artifacts. Known-secret redaction is not
project-data anonymization. Use Ollama or an approved internal gateway for sensitive
data. Proxy/CA environment settings are inherited; MCP-declared variables are shared
with the agent process and listed in the capability metadata.

Re-export after every source or case edit. Use expected.model.path to select a
saved output when a workflow generates several OSM files; server intermediates are
kept as evidence. The report labels SI's weighted score separately from native
DeepEval per-metric pass. Both are advisory.

The optional plugin/evals/calibration/judge-injection-canary.json exercises a judge
with a deliberately wrong answer containing scoring instructions. Run eval with
--cases ./plugin/evals/calibration in a separate output directory and inspect the
actual answer. A low correctness score is intended. JSON evidence boundaries and
grading rules reduce injection exposure, but each real provider/model needs local
calibration. This canary is not part of the default cases or CI.
